From: firepinn <4785534+firepinn@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rk3506_rproc: fix kernel panic on module unload
Upstream-Status: Pending

rmmod rk3506_rproc oopses and panics the board.

rk3506_rproc_remove() stops the core, unmaps every register window, and only
then calls rproc_del(). But rproc_del() shuts the remote processor down if it
is still booted -- and with auto_boot it always is -- which calls back into
.stop, which writes PMU_INT_MASK_CON through mcu->regs_PMU. By then that
mapping is gone, so the write lands on an unmapped address and takes an oops.
With panic=10 on the kernel command line the board reboots; without it, it
just dies.

Call rproc_del() first, while the mappings it depends on are still valid, and
unmap afterwards. Also drop the direct rk3506_rproc_shutdown() call: it stops
the core, and rproc_del() already does that through the proper path, so the
old code stopped it twice. It stays registered as the platform driver's
.shutdown callback for system shutdown.

This matters beyond tidiness. A fresh probe *loads* the firmware, where a
stop/start only restarts the image already resident in the TCM (see
0002-restart-without-reloading-tcm.patch), so rmmod/insmod is the one way to
put *different* firmware on the M0 without rebooting -- and it was unusable.

Signed-off-by: firepinn <4785534+firepinn@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/rk3506_rproc.c
+++ b/rk3506_rproc.c
@@ -604,7 +604,26 @@
 
   device_remove_file(&pdev->dev, &dev_attr_m0_status);
 
-  rk3506_rproc_shutdown(pdev);
+  /*
+   * Order matters, and getting it wrong panics the machine.
+   *
+   * rproc_del() shuts the remote processor down if it is still booted -- and
+   * with auto_boot it always is -- which calls back into our .stop, which
+   * writes PMU_INT_MASK_CON through mcu->regs_PMU. So rproc_del() has to run
+   * while those mappings are still valid.
+   *
+   * The original order stopped the core, unmapped every register window, and
+   * only then called rproc_del(). The shutdown inside rproc_del() then wrote
+   * to an unmapped address: rmmod took a kernel oops and the board panicked
+   * and rebooted. That also made rmmod/insmod useless as a way to load
+   * different M0 firmware, which is the one thing it is good for -- a fresh
+   * probe reloads instead of restarting the resident image.
+   *
+   * rk3506_rproc_shutdown() is not called here either: it stops the core, and
+   * rproc_del() already does that through the proper path. It stays as the
+   * platform driver's .shutdown callback for system shutdown.
+   */
+  rproc_del(rproc);
 
   if (mcu->tcm_virt)    iounmap(mcu->tcm_virt);
   if (mcu->shmem_virt)  iounmap(mcu->shmem_virt);
@@ -613,7 +632,6 @@
   if (mcu->regs_CRU)    iounmap(mcu->regs_CRU);
   if (mcu->regs_GRF)    iounmap(mcu->regs_GRF);
 
-  rproc_del(rproc);
   rproc_free(rproc);
   return 0;
 }
